The board state has no immediate winning move for o (Player o). Exhaustive checking of all seven legal drop positions confirms that none complete a line of four o's.

The opponent (x), however, has a forced win against any move other than column 1. The unique forcing sequence begins with x dropping in column 1, producing three consecutive x's on row 1:

- that placement simultaneously creates an immediate threat at the already-legal cell (1,0) and a latent threat at (1,4);
- o is compelled to answer at (1,0);
- x then makes two successive drops in column 4, each time opening the next higher cell;
- the second of those drops opens the diagonal threat cell (2,4) that already contains three x's, forcing o to block there;
- gravity now makes (1,4) legal on x's following turn, and x completes four-in-a-row on row 1.

Exactly the same terminal sequence is available to x after any first move by o except the move that occupies column 1 itself. Consequently every column other than 1 is a loss under optimal replies.

Occupying column 1 with o prevents the three-in-a-row on row 1 from ever being formed. With that threat eliminated, x possesses no comparable forced win, the special parity/gravity relationship that previously linked the diagonal threat at (2,4) to the horizontal threat at (1,4) disappears, and the game continues from a roughly equal (though still complex) mid-game position.

No other first-move candidate denies the forced win, nor does any alternative create a counter-threat that would let o ignore x's column-1 occupation. Column 1 is therefore the unique optimal choice.

Final Answer: 1

